## Part 1 — first deploy, start to finish

Nine steps, in order. Steps 0–3 are once per machine / once per project; 4–8 are
the deploy.

### Step 0 — install two things

| Tool | Why | Install |
| ---- | --- | ------- |
| **Docker** | `dude iac` runs terraform/kubectl/helm/k9s inside a container, so you don't install them | [docs.docker.com](https://docs.docker.com/get-docker/) |
| **Azure CLI** (`az`) | signing in needs a browser, which a container doesn't have | `brew install azure-cli` · [other platforms](https://learn.microsoft.com/cli/azure/install-azure-cli) |

That is the whole list. You do **not** need terraform, kubectl, helm or k9s on
your machine.

### Step 1 — have the right access in Azure

You need an Azure subscription where your account can:

- **create resource groups** (role: *Contributor*), and
- **assign roles** (role: *User Access Administrator*).

*Owner* covers both. The role assignment matters because the cluster is granted
`AcrPull` on the container registry — that is what lets pods pull images without
a Kubernetes pull secret. If your account can't assign roles, `dude iac apply`
fails on `azurerm_role_assignment.acr_pull`; ask whoever administers the
subscription for *User Access Administrator*, or to pre-create the assignment.

Check which subscriptions you can see:

```bash
az login
az account list --output table
```

### Step 2 — sign in for this environment

```bash
dude iac login --env dev
```

This runs `az login` if you aren't signed in, then prints the subscription the
`dev` environment will deploy into, and the id to pin it with. Pin it — an
unpinned environment silently follows whatever `az account set` was run last:

```hcl
# iac/terraform/environments/dev/terraform.tfvars
subscription_id = "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"
```

> On a machine with no browser, use `az login --use-device-code` first, then
> re-run `dude iac login --env dev`.

### Step 3 — create the shared resources (once per project)

```bash
dude iac bootstrap --state-prefix <yourorg> --location westeurope --env dev --yes
```

This creates, in a resource group `{{projectName}}-shared-rg`:

- a **storage account** + `tfstate` blob container, holding the Terraform remote
  state for *every* environment, and
- an **Azure Container Registry**, holding the app images for *every* environment.

then writes their names into `environments/dev/{backend.hcl,terraform.tfvars}` for
you. No copy-paste.

**About `--state-prefix`**: storage account and registry names are globally
unique across all of Azure and allow only lowercase letters and digits — no
hyphens, 24 characters max for the storage account. Use a short org slug
(`acme`, `unicc`). If the name is taken you'll get a clear error; pick another
prefix and re-run.

You run this **once per project**, not once per environment.

### Step 4 — initialise Terraform

```bash
dude iac init --env dev
```

Points Terraform at the remote state created in step 3.

### Step 5 — provision the infrastructure

```bash
dude iac plan  --env dev     # review what will be created
dude iac apply --env dev     # create it (add --yes to skip the prompt)
```

**This takes around 10 minutes.** The AKS cluster is most of it; the load
balancer and its public IP add a couple of minutes at the end — `apply`
deliberately waits for them, so that when it returns the hostname already
resolves.

What you get: a resource group, a virtual network, an AKS cluster with an
autoscaling node pool, ingress-nginx behind a public IP with an Azure DNS
label{{#if withPostgres}}, and a private PostgreSQL Flexible Server{{/if}}.

### Step 6 — point kubectl at the cluster

```bash
dude iac kubeconfig --env dev
```

### Step 7 — ship the application

```bash
dude iac ship --env dev
```

Builds both production images, pushes them to the registry, and runs
`helm upgrade --install`. The registry host, the image tag and the public
hostname are wired in automatically.

### Step 8 — open it

```bash
dude iac output --env dev        # look for app_url
dude iac status --env dev        # helm release + pods
```

```
http://{{projectName}}-dev.westeurope.cloudapp.azure.com/
```

The API is served under `/api`{{#if withPostgres}}; database migrations ran as a
Helm pre-upgrade hook{{/if}}.

**If the page doesn't load yet**, give the load balancer a minute and check the
pods are up:

```bash
dude iac status --env dev
dude iac shell --env dev      # then: kubectl describe ingress -n dev
```

