## The IaC runner (Docker)

`dude iac *` runs terraform / kubectl / helm / k9s inside a pinned container so
you don't have to install or version-match any of them locally. The image is
built on first use from `iac/runner/Dockerfile` — **which is yours to edit** (bump
a tool version, add a CLI). The image is tagged by a hash of that file, so any
change rebuilds automatically on the next command.

- **Credentials** are never copied in: your `~/.azure` is mounted and the
  environment's subscription is passed through, so your `az login` session keeps
  working. That mount is read-**write** on purpose — the Azure CLI rewrites its
  token cache when it refreshes an access token, and a read-only mount breaks
  authentication as soon as the cached token expires. `~/.kube` is mounted
  read-only as a fallback context.
- **`dude iac login`** is the one command that stays on the host (it needs a
  browser); it writes the token the container then reads.
- **`dude iac build`/`push`** use the host Docker daemon (they build app images);
  everything else runs in the container.
- **Escape hatch**: set `DUDE_IAC_RUNNER=host` to run the tools natively from
  your PATH instead. If Docker isn't running, `dude iac` falls back to native
  tools automatically.
- **`dude iac shell --env <env>`** drops you into the runner with all tools + k9s,
  the env's subscription and cluster already wired — handy for ad-hoc inspection.

## Subscriptions per environment

Each `dude iac …` command is environment-scoped via `--env`, which is
**required** — there is no default, so you never act on the wrong environment by
accident. Run any command without it to list the environments discovered under
`iac/terraform/environments/`. Each env resolves an **Azure subscription**, so
different environments can live in different subscriptions. The precedence:

1. an explicit `--subscription <id>` flag,
2. `subscription_id` in the environment's `terraform.tfvars`,
3. an already-exported `ARM_SUBSCRIPTION_ID` / `AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID`,
4. whatever `az account show` reports as your signed-in default.

Pin it at level 2 for anything beyond a first experiment: it is the only level
that is committed, reviewed, and the same for everyone on the team.

## Build, push & deploy

`dude` drives the whole release. The registry is read from Terraform outputs and
the image tag defaults to the **git short SHA** (with a `-dirty-<hash>` suffix if
you have uncommitted changes), so every deploy is traceable to an exact commit.
Override with `--tag <tag>` (e.g. `--tag v1.2.3`).

The fast inner loop:

```bash
dude iac ship   --env dev    # build → push → helm upgrade --install
dude iac status --env dev    # release status + pods
```

Or run the stages individually (handy in CI, where each is a separate stage):

```bash
dude iac build  --env dev        # docker build BE+FE (linux/amd64) for the registry
dude iac push   --env dev        # ACR login + docker push BE+FE
dude iac deploy --env dev        # helm upgrade --install (auto-sets registry, tag, host)
```

> **Platform** — images are built for `linux/amd64` by default to match the AKS
> x86 node pool. Building on Apple Silicon without this flag yields arm64 images
> that crash on the cluster with `exec format error`. Override with `--platform`
> if you move the node pool to Arm (`Standard_D2pds_v5` and friends).

> **Overwriting a tag** — unlike ECR, a Basic-tier Azure registry lets a tag be
> rewritten. `push`/`ship` warn when the tag already exists rather than refusing,
> since re-shipping the same working tree is legitimate. To *promote* an existing
> image instead of rebuilding it, use `dude iac deploy --env <env> --tag <tag>`.

`deploy` injects `image.registry`, the image tags and `ingress.host` via `--set`,
so you do not hand-edit them into `values-dev.yaml` — that file is still layered
for the rest (resources, replicas{{#if withPostgres}}, secrets{{/if}}…).

> **Config & secrets** — every app container loads its env via `envFrom` from a
> ConfigMap (`.Values.config`) and a Secret (`.Values.secrets`). Each key is the
> ENV VAR NAME exactly as `app/core/config.py` reads it, so adding a variable is
> one line under `config:` / `secrets:` plus the field in `config.py`. Per-env
> `values-<env>.yaml` files are **gitignored** (they may hold secrets like API
> keys); the committed `values.yaml` is the documented reference.

{{#if withPostgres}}`secrets.DATABASE_URL` must be present (the chart refuses to deploy otherwise),
but you don't set it by hand: `dude iac ship`/`deploy` injects it via
`--set-string` from the Terraform `database_url` output. The database has **no
public endpoint** — it is reachable only from inside the VNet, which is why
`psql` from your laptop won't connect. To reach it, port-forward through a pod or
use `dude iac shell`.

{{/if}}## Add a new environment

```bash
dude iac new-env --env prod          # copies dev by default; use --from <env> to pick the source
```

This scaffolds `iac/terraform/environments/prod/{backend.hcl,terraform.tfvars}`,
setting `environment = "prod"`, a per-env state `key`, and a fresh `dns_label`
(left at the source env's value, the new environment would collide with — or
quietly take over — the old one's URL). The storage account, the state container
**and the registry** are **shared** across environments, so you don't bootstrap
again unless this is the first env on a brand-new backend. It also copies the
source env's gitignored `iac/helm/app/values-<from>.yaml` to `values-prod.yaml`
when present, so the new env starts from the same Helm overrides + secrets
instead of the bare `values.yaml` defaults.

Then edit `environments/prod/terraform.tfvars` (bigger nodes,{{#if withPostgres}} a
General Purpose `db_sku_name`, `db_high_availability = true`,{{/if}} `sku_tier`,
…) and `iac/helm/app/values-prod.yaml` (replicas, autoscaling, secrets, …), and
run every command with `--env prod`:

```bash
dude iac init --env prod && dude iac apply --env prod && dude iac ship --env prod
```

## Local vs CI/CD

The exact same commands run in CI — state is remote (Azure Blob Storage) and auth
is the Azure CLI/SDK chain, so no kubeconfig file is committed. In a pipeline,
authenticate with a service principal or workload identity federation by
exporting `ARM_CLIENT_ID`, `ARM_TENANT_ID`, `ARM_SUBSCRIPTION_ID` and either
`ARM_CLIENT_SECRET` or OIDC; the Terraform provider picks them up with no flag
changes. A pipeline typically runs: `dude iac init/plan/apply` → `dude iac build`
→ `dude iac push` → `dude iac deploy` (or `dude iac ship` to collapse the last
three). Pass an explicit `--tag $CI_COMMIT_SHA` so the pipeline controls
provenance.

## Tear down

```bash
dude iac destroy --env dev
```

`destroy` tears down **everything** for the environment, in order:

1. uninstalls the Helm release and waits for the Ingress to go, so the ingress
   controller releases the Azure load balancer and public IP cleanly;
2. `terraform destroy` of the environment — which removes its whole resource
   group, cluster{{#if withPostgres}}, database{{/if}} and network;
3. re-checks that the resource group is actually gone (a delete still in flight,
   or a resource lock, otherwise leaves it quietly billing);
4. tears down the bootstrap — the shared state storage account **and** the shared
   registry — but **only if no other environment still uses it** (see below).

Flags for finer control:

| Flag             | Effect                                                              |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `--keep-backend` | Keep the shared bootstrap (state storage account **+ container registry**). It is kept automatically while another environment still uses it; pass this to force-keep it even when destroying the last environment. |
| `--skip-helm`    | Skip the Helm uninstall (the release is already gone).              |
| `--skip-tf`      | Skip the per-env `terraform destroy` — only run the Helm uninstall + backend teardown. |

> The bootstrap holds the project-wide shared resources — the Terraform state
> storage account **and** the container registry — used by every environment
> (they differ only by their state `key`). To protect the other environments'
> state *and registry*, `destroy` automatically **keeps** the bootstrap whenever
> another environment is **still provisioned**, and only tears it down when
> you're destroying the **last** one. Whether a sibling is "still provisioned" is
> read from its remote Terraform state (empty after a destroy), not from its
> folder on disk — so a leftover env folder you already destroyed won't keep the
> backend alive. Pass `--keep-backend` to force-keep it even then.

## Commands

| Command | Wraps |
|---------|-------|
| `dude iac login`     | `az login` (when needed) + `az account show` for the env's subscription |
| `dude iac new-env`   | scaffolds `environments/<name>/` from an existing env (`--env <name>`, `--from <src>`) |
| `dude iac bootstrap` | `terraform init/apply` in `bootstrap/`, patches `backend.hcl` + `terraform.tfvars` |
| `dude iac init`      | `terraform init` (azurerm backend per env) |
| `dude iac plan`      | `terraform plan` |
| `dude iac apply`     | `terraform apply` (`--yes` to auto-approve) |
| `dude iac destroy`   | helm uninstall + `terraform destroy` + bootstrap teardown (`--yes`, `--keep-backend`, `--skip-helm`, `--skip-tf`) |
| `dude iac output`    | `terraform output` (`--json`) |
| `dude iac fmt`       | `terraform fmt -recursive` |
| `dude iac validate`  | `terraform validate` |
| `dude iac build`     | `docker build` BE+FE prod images (`linux/amd64`) |
| `dude iac push`      | ACR token login + `docker push` BE+FE |
| `dude iac kubeconfig`| `az aks get-credentials` from outputs |
| `dude iac deploy`    | `helm upgrade --install` (auto-sets registry + tag + host{{#if withPostgres}} + DATABASE_URL{{/if}}) |
| `dude iac ship`      | `build` + `push` + `deploy` |
| `dude iac status`    | `helm status` + `kubectl get pods` |
| `dude iac shell`     | interactive shell in the IaC runner container (terraform, kubectl, helm, k9s, az) |

All commands take `--env <name>` (**required**; autodiscovered from
`environments/*`) and `--subscription <id>` (overrides the env's resolved
subscription). `build`/`push`/`deploy`/`ship` take `--tag <tag>` (default: git
short SHA, `-dirty-<hash>` if uncommitted); `build`/`ship` also take `--platform`
(default `linux/amd64`). `bootstrap` accepts `--state-prefix` (required) and
`--location` (default `westeurope`).

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Cause / fix |
| ------- | ----------- |
| `apply` fails on `azurerm_role_assignment.acr_pull` with *AuthorizationFailed* | Your account can't assign roles. Ask for **User Access Administrator** (or Owner) on the subscription. |
| `apply` fails: DNS label already in use | Another Azure resource in that region took it. Change `dns_label` in the env's `terraform.tfvars` and re-apply. |
| `bootstrap` fails: storage account name taken | The name is global across Azure. Re-run with a different `--state-prefix`. |
| App URL doesn't resolve | The load balancer and its DNS label take a couple of minutes after `apply`. Re-check `dude iac output --env <env>`. |
| Pods stuck in `ImagePullBackOff` | The `AcrPull` role assignment hasn't propagated yet (up to a few minutes), or `apply` never created it. `dude iac shell --env <env>` then `kubectl describe pod <pod> -n <env>`. |
| `init` fails with *AuthorizationPermissionMismatch* | You switched the backend to `use_azuread_auth`. That needs the data-plane role **Storage Blob Data Contributor** on the state storage account — subscription Owner alone is not enough. Grant it, or drop the setting (see `backend.hcl`). |
| `Error acquiring the state lock` | A previous command died mid-run. `dude iac shell --env <env>`, then `terraform -chdir=iac/terraform force-unlock <id>`. |
{{#if withPostgres}}| `psql` from your laptop can't connect | By design: the database has no public endpoint and lives inside the VNet. Reach it through a pod, or `dude iac shell`. |
{{/if}}| `exec format error` in a pod | An arm64 image on an x86 node pool. Rebuild without overriding `--platform`. |
